es of Japanese green tea with a toasty, slightly caramel-like flavour. The roasting process used to make hÅjicha also lowers the amount of caffeine in the tea. Because of its mildness, hÅjicha is a popular tea to serve during the evening meal, before sleep, and preferred for children and the elderly. [ 5 ] See also Bancha Japanese tea Kukicha References ^ A Beginner's Guide to Japanese Tea: Selecting and Brewing the Perfect Cup of Sencha, Matcha, and Other Japanese Teas . Tuttle Publishing. 2002. p. 28. ISBN 978-4805316382 . ^ Uhl, Joseph Wesley (2015). The Art and Craft of Tea: An Enthusiast's Guide to Selecting, Brewing, and Serving Exquisite Tea . Quarry Books. p. 33. ISBN 978-1631590498 . OCLC 1232411381 . ^ Driem, George L. van (2019-01-14). The Tale of Tea: A Comprehensive History of Tea from Prehistoric Times to the Present Day . BRILL. ISBN 978-90-04-39360-8 . ^ Kilby, Nick (2015). The Book of Tea: All Things Tea . Jacqui Small LLP. p. 248. ISBN 978-1909342842 . ^ Saberi, Helen (2010). Tea: A Global History . Reaktion Books. p. 48. ISBN 978-1861897763 . OCLC 908338259 . Japanese food and drink Shushoku Rice Chahan Curry Katsu curry Glutinous Hatsuga genmai Kayu Donmono ChÅ«kadon GyÅ«don Katsudon Oyakodon Tekkadon Tenshindon Unadon Hayashi rice Okowa Onigiri Tenmusu Omurice Sushi History Sustainable Sekihan Takikomi Zosui Noodles Harusame Hiyamugi Hiyashi chÅ«ka Ramen Champon Kagoshima Muroran curry Tantan-men Tonkotsu Tsukemen Shirataki Soba Okinawa soba Soki SÅmen Salad Udon Yakiudon Yakisoba HÅtÅ Konamono Monjayaki Okonomiyaki Hirayachi Takoyaki Bread Anpan Curry Melonpan Milk bread Yudane Okazu Agedashi dÅfu Agemono Furai Karaage Korokke Kushikatsu Satsuma-age Tempura Chawanmushi Chikuzenni Gari GyÅza Kabayaki Kakuni Kushiyaki Nabemono Namasu NattÅ Nikujaga Oden Onsen tamago Sashimi Fugusashi Shabu-shabu Shiokara Sukiyaki Tamagoyaki Tokoroten Tonkatsu Tororo Tsukemono Nukazuke Takuan Tsukudani Tsukune Yakiniku Yakitori Soup ( shirumono ) Tonjiru K